WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:03.503
Bladesmiths,
welcome to The Forge.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.772
In past competitions,
we've had smiths come

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.373
<i> and we've asked them
 to layer steel</i>

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.309
<i> so that they could create
 Damascus patterns</i>

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:13.345 line:0%
<i> like Raindrop, Ladder,
 and Sharktooth</i>

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.448 line:0%
And today, we're gonna ask
a similar task of you guys,

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.884
except we don't want just
one of these patterns,

00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:20.286
we want them all.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.122
What we have here
are the bits and pieces

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.257
of pattern Damascus billets

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:29.195
that are Raindrop, Sharktooth,
and Ladder pattern.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.130
And you'll have to
take these bits,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.465
<i> load them into the cans,</i>

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:34.200
<i> combine that
 with powdered steel</i>

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:37.536 line:0%
<i> to create a
 Fossil Damascus billet</i>

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:40.606 line:0%
<i> from which you will forge
 your signature blades</i>

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:41.841 line:0%
<i> in your signature style.</i>

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:42.975
Good luck, gentlemen.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:44.611
Your three hours starts... now.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.846
How difficult is this challenge?

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:50.182
Honestly, you've got really good
steels you're working with.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.017
The biggest challenge
is packing

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:53.619
everything into that can.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.822
With all these different-sized
pieces and shapes,

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:57.857
they've got to make sure
that they're filling in

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.092
all the gaps
with that 1095 powder

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:01.994
or they're just not gonna
get a good weld.

00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:05.564
The only way I have any clue
on how to do Canister Damascus

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.133
<i> is from watching a video
 that J. Nielson put out,</i>

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.536
so I'm gonna try my best
to do what he did.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:14.074
J. used Wite-Out,
I'm gonna use Wite-Out.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.014
J. inset his top cap
on the canister,

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:23.116
I'm gonna inset my top cap
on the canister.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.151
Smart work. It actually
makes the canister

00:01:26.251 --> 00:01:28.087
a lot more structurally strong.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:31.291
[Jesse]<i> I decide to go
 with the small cubes.</i>

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.659
My thought is
it's less material per one

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:34.793
and it'll heat up faster.

00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:36.296
<i> And from my experience,</i>

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.498
<i> the smaller piece
 is actually easier to compact</i>

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:40.099
<i> and make it one
 solid billet.</i>

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.667
[Wil] Why would you peel
two cans

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:42.901
when you can peel one?

00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:44.203
Doesn't make any sense to me.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.439
I have one canister in the forge
right now heating up

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:48.474
while I'm getting
the other one prepped.

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:49.975
<i> That way we'll get
 a cycle going</i>

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.177
<i> so both of 'em
 will be good.</i>

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.712
<i> Take 'em apart,
 forge weld 'em together,</i>

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:53.713
<i> should have a knife.</i>

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.817
I am notorious
for working messy.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.218
God, it's a mess.

00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:03.056
Wilson's, like, smearing
Wite-Out all over everything.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:05.024
[J.] Like a van Gogh painting
over there.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.426
What are you doing
to our table over there, man?!

00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:08.995
-I'll clean it up!
-All right.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.463
[Jesse]<i> So I take
 my super hot canister</i>

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.632
<i> over to the squaring dies
 on the hydraulic press.</i>

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.668
<i> And I want to squish it down
 a little at a time,</i>

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:19.238
<i> and I'm gonna keep going
 until this is solid.</i>

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:21.106
That's how you do it.
That's how you set your welds.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.175
Get that square can,
you're pushing all four sides

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.244
at the same time,
compressing everything evenly.

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:29.248
[Wil]<i> Teddy has two small
 canisters he's dealing with.</i>

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:30.983
[Teddy]<i> So I grab the first can,</i>

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.818
just smash, smash, smash.

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:36.555
The second one, same thing.
I'm excited.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.692
<i> I can tell by the way it's
 smashing it's being spill set,</i>

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:40.894
<i> it's gonna be good.</i>

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:43.530
I can't wait any longer.

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:44.897
Here goes nothin'!

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:46.098
Whoo! That's hot!

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.834
<i> I give it a good press
 all the way down,</i>

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:49.903
I turn it 90 degrees,

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.671
<i> do another press
 all the way down,</i>

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:53.873
I do a pass on the Big BLU.

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.341
That rod is hot
as a two-dollar pistol!

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.011
<i> Normally at home,
 I'd do at least two passes</i>

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:00.212
<i> on the squaring dies,</i>

00:03:00.345 --> 00:03:03.182
but I go with my gut
that it's a good weld.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.417
-Oh, that can is hot!
-[whirring]

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.320
[David] I don't believe
that those welds

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.622
are consolidated
in that billet he's got

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.724
after one run on the press.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.492
[muttering indistinctly]

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.861
Wilson's still got problems
up in that blade

00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:17.430
from that initial weld.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.466
[Doug] So if Wilson's billet
does have some cracks in it,

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.368
is it best to chop them up

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.170
and try to forge weld 'em
all over again?

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.272
[J.] I'd try to grind them out.
Just get rid of 'em.

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.641
Make 'em disappear,
then you can stretch it out.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.277
[Wilson]<i> I know that
 I can't grind it out.</i>

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.880
To me, my only option is
to try to weld it.

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:37.416
[Wil] We got Wilson over
here welding,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.319
and welding is... a bad thing
at this point.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.888
It's not a good sign.

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:46.058
Man, Jesse's got
a cool-lookin' blade.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.360
Yeah, I like it.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.829
<i> And we got a quenched blade.</i>

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:54.367
Teddy quenched.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.936
<i> We have three quenched blades
 out there on The Forge floor.</i>

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:58.704
Bladesmiths!
Shut down your machines!

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:02.875
Drop your tools! This first
round of competition is over!

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:06.078
Bladesmiths, welcome
to the strength test.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.813
We got the bone chop here.

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:12.051
I'ma take each of your blades
and I'm gonna baton them

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.854
through these very large,
very dense bones.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.424
What your blades do to the bones
I'm not too concerned about.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.660
I wanna see what the bones
do to your blades.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.128
Teddy, you ready
to rock and roll?

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:24.664
-Let's do this.
-Let's do it.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.799
[intense music playing]

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:28.801
[grunts]

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.337
[grunts]

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.274
[grunts]

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:37.677
[grunts]

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.349
[tense music playing]

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.919
[J.] Good job, Teddy.
Your edge is fine.

00:04:46.985 --> 00:04:51.189
There's a couple tiny little
glints I can see on the edge.

00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:54.192
-But overall, good job.
-Thank you.

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:55.961
-How you feeling, Jesse?
-I'm ready.

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.629
-Good, so am I.
-Go nuts.

00:04:57.796 --> 00:04:59.965
-I'm halfway there already.
-[laughter]

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:01.768
[intense music playing]

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.572
[grunts]

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.574
[grunts]

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:10.943
[grunts]

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.378
Oh, my gosh.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:18.851
[grunts]

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:22.989
[chuckles]

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.726
-Nice job, Jesse.
-Thank you.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.395
You did lose a little bit
of your fine edge,

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:32.565
but overall,
knife's fairly comfortable.

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:34.900
-You survived. Good job.
-Thank you.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.368
How you feeling, Wilson?

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:39.472
I'm ready for you to do
whatever J. does.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:41.173
This is what I do.

00:05:41.306 --> 00:05:42.742
Then do it to it.

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:48.348
-[grunts]
-[intense music playing]

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.016
[grunts]

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:51.818
[grunts]

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:57.423
[grunts]

00:05:59.491 --> 00:06:00.860
-Yes!
-Unbelievable.

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:02.362
[laughs]

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:07.967
Good job, Wilson.

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:10.436
-You made it through.
-Made it through. [chuckles]

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:13.239
[J.] The only thing I see,
there's not much edge

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:14.474
pretty much from here up.

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:15.340
Okay.

00:06:15.407 --> 00:06:17.143
But striking the bone...

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.580
didn't roll anything,
didn't chip it out.

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:22.648
-Good job.
-Thank you.

00:06:23.148 --> 00:06:24.384
Dave.

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:28.587
Gentlemen, you've survived
our strength test.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.890
Now onto the sharpness test.
The apple slice.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.092
Unlike the strength test,
this is all about

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:35.327
what your blades do
to those apples.

00:06:35.427 --> 00:06:37.396
Looking for a good, clean cut.

00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:38.497
Teddy, you're up first. Ready?

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.400
-Let's do it.
-[David] All right.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:46.406
[dramatic music playing]

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:56.948
[chuckles] Man.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:58.084
[softly] Fudge.

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:01.753
So, Teddy, your knife is
definitely sharp.

00:07:01.853 --> 00:07:04.990
But if you look at the tip,
it's kinda got a warp this way.

00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:07.326
It's flat on one side,
it's ground on the other.

00:07:08.027 --> 00:07:09.728
When I hit those apples,
you can actually see it

00:07:09.761 --> 00:07:11.430
in the apples,
it just goes "swish,"

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.499
and rides up right up
out of 'em.

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:15.434
It affects the cut,
but it doesn't affect the fact

00:07:15.568 --> 00:07:17.436
that this is still
a sharp blade.

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:19.071
-Well done.
-Thank you.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:20.572
All right, Jesse,
you're up next.

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:22.107
-You ready for this?
-Yes, sir.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:23.443
[David] Okay.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.377
[sharp exhale]

00:07:25.477 --> 00:07:26.979
[intense music playing]

00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:34.987
Man.

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:40.526
All right, Jesse.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:42.160
There's some damage
to this edge.

00:07:42.294 --> 00:07:44.062
It's a really obtuse edge.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:45.464
You can see that the slice

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.233
kinda goes halfway through
that apple

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:50.703
and it cracks it off after that
'cause the blade gets so wide.

00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:53.572
Not the sharpest thing
on the planet, but it'll do it.

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:55.340
-Good job.
-Thank you.

00:07:55.373 --> 00:07:58.110
All right, Wilson. You're up.

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:00.213
-Cut it.
-[chuckles] Okay.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:04.951
[intense music playing]

00:08:12.792 --> 00:08:13.893
[snickers]

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:15.895
So, yeah, it is sharp.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.663
But it's just wedging
them apart.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.899
And yeah, I could probably chop
wood with this,

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.333
but for slicing apples,

00:08:21.433 --> 00:08:23.469
it's just got the wrong
edge geometry.

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:24.704
I understand.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:29.108
[Wil] Bladesmiths, the judges'
deliberation is complete.

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:30.609
They've made their
final decision.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:34.479
It's time for one of you smiths
to leave The Forge.

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:36.315
The Bladesmith leaving
The Forge is...

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:38.784
[tense music playing]

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:42.487
Wilson, your blade
didn't make the cut

00:08:42.587 --> 00:08:44.123
and David Baker's gonna
tell you why.

00:08:44.223 --> 00:08:48.026
Wilson, your blade's heavy,
it has an obtuse edge,

00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:49.995
and it was really designed
only for one of our tests.

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:52.531
But before we let you go,
I wanna tell you

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:55.467
that rarely
have we seen a smith

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.603
work so hard to get around
what looked like

00:08:57.703 --> 00:08:59.405
insurmountable problems.

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:00.772
You pushed to the very end,

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:02.308
made it through that
strength test,

00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:04.309
and I'm just really proud
of what you did.

00:09:04.376 --> 00:09:05.545
Thank you.

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:08.548
Wilson, please surrender
your blade.

00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:13.452
[Wilson]<i> This has been
 an excellent experience.</i>

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:16.354
It's showed me that I have
the drive to keep going

00:09:16.421 --> 00:09:18.557
and going
even when things

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.426
<i> are not turning out right</i>

00:09:20.492 --> 00:09:22.661
<i> and are not gonna
 turn out right.</i>

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:24.630
<i> But you have to do
 the best you can</i>

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.098
and keep going at it.

